# Project rules

You are building a self-hosted hackathon submission and judging portal. The full specification is
`docs/SPEC.md`. Read the relevant section before writing code, and follow it exactly.

## Hard rules
- Everything must work offline after `docker compose up`. No cloud services, CDNs, external fonts,
  or runtime network calls.
- Every permission rule is enforced in the backend (middleware or service). Hiding UI is extra,
  never the protection.
- Time rules use `phase.ts` and `clock.now()`. Never trust client time.
- Do not edit `fixtures.json`, `run.py`, or `LICENSE`.
- Do not invent fixture fields. Read `fixtures.json` to see the real shape.
- Do not change `schema.prisma`, the API contract, or the error format without updating
  `docs/SPEC.md` in the same change.

## Stack
TypeScript strict · Express 5 · Prisma + PostgreSQL 16 · Zod · bcryptjs · React 18 + Vite ·
React Router 6 · TanStack Query 5 · Tailwind · Vitest + Supertest. Do not add other frameworks,
UI kits, auth libraries, state managers, WebSockets, or Redis.

## Code style
- Routes are thin: validate → authorize → call service → respond. Services hold the rules and
  throw `HttpError`. No try/catch in routes.
- Algorithms are pure functions with no DB access: `normalization.ts`, `assignment.ts`, `phase.ts`,
  `duplicates.ts`, `csv.ts`.
- Error body is always `{ "error": { "code", "message", "details?" } }`.
- Every state change writes an audit row via `audit()`.
- No `any`, no `@ts-ignore`, no unused code, no console.log (except seed summary and server start).
- Comments explain why, never what. No commented-out code, no TODOs, no decorative comments.
- Clear names, small files, named constants instead of magic numbers.
- Frontend: only `api/client.ts` calls fetch. Every page handles loading, empty and error states.
  Keep the UI simple: Tailwind, white background, slate text, indigo-600 accent, plain tables and
  one-column forms.

## Before finishing any task
Run `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint` and `npm test`, and fix everything they report.
